<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Puxian Wannu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. after 1233</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Puxian Wannu was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchen</a> general of the Jin dynasty who was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a> to fight the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> and Khitan rebels. In 1215 he broke with the Jin and founded his own state, later called Eastern Xia, in the region of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tumen">Tumen</a> river. The Mongols captured him in 1233 and destroyed his state.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/puxian-wannu.jpg" alt="Puxian Wannu" width="320" height="374" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:P%C3%BAxi%C4%81n_W%C3%A0nn%C3%BA.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Puxian Wannu was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchen</a> from Xianping. He first served the Jin as Director of the Shangjiu Bureau, in charge of the horses of the court and the army. In 1206 he was deputy to the Grand Commander Wanyan Saibu in the Jin invasion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a>, and at Zhenshui he led his troops round to strike the Song army from the rear; some twenty thousand Song troops were killed. The Jin ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-zhangzong-of-jin">Wanyan Jing</a> then made him Pacification Commissioner of Xianping and sent him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaodong">Liaodong</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> invaded in 1211, the Jin army was routed at Yehu Ridge, and Puxian Wannu withdrew to Xianping. The Jin court moved its capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a>, and Khitan leaders such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yelu-liuge">Yelü Liuge</a> rose in the northeast proclaiming the restoration of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao</a>. Ordered repeatedly to suppress them, he won some battles and lost others. In 1215, the year the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhongdu">Zhongdu</a>, he called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchen</a> chieftains to a council at Xianping, argued that the court could no longer defend <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaodong">Liaodong</a>, and with their support declared independence as Heavenly King of a state named Great Zhen, with the reign title Tiantai.</p><p>In 1217 he moved to the Tumen River basin, renamed his state Eastern Xia and set his capital, called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a>, around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yanji">Yanji</a>. His domain reached from the Laoyeling Mountains south into present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-korea">North Korea</a> and east to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-japan">Sea of Japan</a>. He seems to have submitted to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> again, and in 1218 Mongol and Eastern Xia armies entered Goryeo together to destroy the Khitan remnants.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In 1222, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> was campaigning in the west, he revolted against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> once more, and after Goryeo refused to open trading posts on the border he invaded it several times. In 1233 Güyük, the son of Ögedei, attacked Eastern Xia with a large army and took five of its capitals in succession. With his fall the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchen</a> remnants in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a> surrendered, beginning almost two hundred years of Mongol rule there. Persian sources such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jami">Jami</a>' al-tawarikh mention him under another name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Puxian Wannu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Puxian Wannu</b>, a Jin dynasty (1115-1234) commander in Liaodong, was sent against the rebel Yelü Liuge of Eastern Liao but was defeated.</blockquote><a href="/state/eastern-xia">Eastern Xia</a></li></ul></section>
